y_factors": ["Begich is an incumbent with a large fundraising edge ($5.5M raised, $3.1M on hand)", "Raters overwhelmingly call the race Likely R, with only two at Lean R", "Democratic nominee Eric Hafner is a little-known, low-funded challenger", "Alaska's R+6 lean and a favorable environment for Republicans"], "rationale": "Every structural advantage points to Begich: incumbency, a dominant war chest, a Republican-leaning state, and a consensus of expert ratings at Likely R or better. Markets price a Democratic win at just 2%, and the strongest recent polling shows Begich near 50% with no serious Democratic counterweight.", "watch": "A late surge by independent Bill Hill, who has raised over $1.3M, could siphon votes in Alaska's ranked-choice system and complicate the final tally.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Nick Begich III (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican incumbent Nick Begich III seeks a full term in Alaska's at-large House seat after advancing from the August primary alongside Democrat Eric Hafner, independent Bill Hill, and a Libertarian. Begich enters with a decisive financial advantage, having raised $5.5 million and holding $3.1 million on hand, while Hafner has reported minimal fundraising. Hill's $1.3 million makes him the best-funded non-major-party challenger, though his path is narrow.\n\nMost national raters place the race at Likely Republican, with a couple at Lean Republican and one at Solid Republican. Available polling shows Begich drawing support in the mid-to-high 40s or above, with no general-election survey showing a competitive Democratic position. Prediction markets give Democrats only a small chance. The seat remains on the Republican side of the ledger, with the main uncertainty being how votes split among the non-Republican candidates.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1234, "margin": -7.11}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1042, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0943, "margin"